Make the orchestrator dispatch each agent by its own name. It now tells the runtime to dispatch
every agent through the general wrapper, which has every tool, so a read-only agent such as
`context` loses its tool limits on that path. Also state the council's delegation rule and
replace three stale phrases.

Files (exactly two, same change in each):
1. .skilled/agents/orchestrate.md
2. .claude/agents/orchestrate.md

Change A. In the `### Agent Selection (Priority Order)` table, the rows with Priority 1 to 8 end
with the cell `"general"`. Replace that last cell with the agent's own name in quotes:
  Priority 1 `"context"`, 2 `"deep-research"`, 3 `"ai-council"`, 4 `"markdown"`,
  5 `"review"`, 6 `"deep-review"`, 7 `"code"`, 8 `"debug"`.
The Priority 9 row and the header row stay as they are.

Change B. In the Priority 1 row, the Skills cell starts `Memory tools, Glob, Grep, Read`.
Replace `Memory tools, Glob, Grep, Read` with `Trigger index lookup, Glob, Grep, Read`.

Change C. Old line:
This Copilot profile enforces **single-hop delegation**. Nested sub-agent dispatch is illegal.
New line:
This orchestrator enforces **single-hop delegation**. Nested sub-agent dispatch is illegal.

Change D. Directly after the `#### Agent Tier Classification` table (after its `| **LEAF** ...` row),
add one blank line and then this line:
The AI Council is a LEAF when another agent dispatches it, and may dispatch council seats only when the operator invokes it directly.

Change E. Under `### 🔒 Agent Loading Protocol (MANDATORY)`. Old line:
3. **SET** `subagent_type: "general-purpose"` (all custom agents use the general subagent type)
New line:
3. **SET** `subagent_type` to the agent's own name, for example `subagent_type: "context"`, so the runtime loads that agent's own tool limits. Use `subagent_type: "general-purpose"` only on a runtime whose delegation tool registers no named agents.

Change F. Under `### Prompt/Agent Consistency Guard (MANDATORY)`. Old line:
3. `subagent_type: "general-purpose"` is only the runtime wrapper; it does not make a mismatched prompt safe.
New line:
3. `subagent_type` MUST name the same agent as the `Agent:` line. The `"general-purpose"` wrapper, used only where a runtime registers no named agents, does not make a mismatched prompt safe.

Change G. In the task-package template. Old line:
├─ Subagent Type: "general" (ALL dispatches use "general" — exploration routes through @context)
New line:
├─ Subagent Type: [the agent's own name, the Agent line without the @ | "general-purpose" only on a runtime with no named agents]

Change H. Old text inside one line:
**Action:** ALWAYS dispatch `@context` (subagent_type: `"general-purpose"`).
New text:
**Action:** ALWAYS dispatch `@context` (subagent_type: `"context"`).
The rest of that line stays.

Change I. Old text inside one line:
Sub-orchestrator fan-out is disabled in this Copilot profile because nested dispatch is illegal.
New text:
Sub-orchestrator fan-out is disabled here because nested dispatch is illegal.
The rest of that line stays.

Leave every `@general-purpose` mention alone. Those name the built-in agent, not a dispatch rule.

